  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nQuiet weather is expected through the next seven days as surface \nhigh pressure remains dominant across the region. Guidance is in \ngood agreement through the weekend, showing upper ridging moving \neastward across the region while surface high pressure centered over \nthe Northeast extends back into central Indiana. Large scale \nsubsidence from these features will continue to keep the forecast \ndry. Light winds combined with mostly clear skies may continue to \npromote patchy ground fog in climatologically favored locations the \nnext few nights. \n\nTemperatures will slightly warm by Sunday due to return flow as \nsurface high pressure shifts further east. An upper trough moving \nacross Canada early next week will attempt to push a weak front into \nthe area. Latest guidance generally shows mean flow becoming parallel \nto the boundary which should stall out front as it approaches. \nMarginal forcing and very limited moisture from the gulf remaining \ncutoff will likely limit any chance for measurable precipitation. A \nslight cooldown of a degrees may be the only noticeable impact from \nthe system, but this will be more due to increasing clouds. Expect \ndry conditions to persist as surface high pressure remains in \ncontrol.\n\nEnsemble guidance begins to diverge towards late next week resulting \nin greater uncertainty. Limited moisture return and weak overall \nforcing should keep weather conditions mostly quiet. A deeper trough \nalong with more substantial moisture advection appears increasingly \nlikely towards the beginning of November which suggest greater \nchances for precipitation. Exact details remain uncertain at this \ntime. Look for highs to generally range from the upper 60s to mid \n70s through the period. Overnight temperatures will mostly fall into \nthe 40s with slightly warmer lows possible towards the middle of \nnext week",
